 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "F_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "From 2013 to 2015, Evaldas Rimasauskas, a Lithuanian citizen, sent fraudulent (phishing) emails with fake invoices to staff of two American internet companies, which wired money in payment; the DOJ did not name them, but a 2017 Lithuanian court order, Fortune/CNBC, Bloomberg and NBC/Reuters identify them as Google and Facebook. Narration: 'He sent Google fake invoices. They paid. Then Facebook paid.' Headline: 'HE EMAILED FAKE INVOICES / GOOGLE AND FACEBOOK PAID'.",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "nbc",
    "cnbc17",
    "bloomberg"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_total",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In total the two companies wired over $100 million (DOJ plea release: 'over $100 million'; DOJ sentencing release title: 'over $120 million'; Bloomberg: $121 million). Narration: 'Over $100 million.' WIRED chip: '$120M+'.",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "doj_sent",
    "bloomberg",
    "npr"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_who",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The scheme's author was Evaldas Rimasauskas, a Lithuanian man (about 50 at his plea), arrested in Lithuania in March 2017 and extradited to the United States in August 2017. Narration: 'The sender was a Lithuanian man.'",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "occrp",
    "nbc",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_supplier",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The real vendor he impersonated was Taiwan-based Quanta Computer, a hardware manufacturer that did business with both companies (it builds servers and other components). Quanta confirmed it was the Asian vendor. Narration: 'Both bought hardware from a real supplier based in Taiwan.'",
   "sources": [
    "nbc",
    "cyberscoop",
    "cnbc17",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_latvia",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He registered a company in Latvia with the same name as the Asian-based computer hardware manufacturer (DOJ). Narration: 'In Latvia, he registered a company with the same name.' Door plaque in the drawing: plain text 'QUANTA COMPUTER' (no logo).",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "nbc",
    "cnbc19",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_accounts",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He opened bank accounts in that company's name at banks in Latvia and Cyprus, into which the victims' payments were wired. Narration: 'But the money went to him.' (accounts he controlled, opened in the look-alike company's name).",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "nbc",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_emails",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He sent phishing emails to employees and agents of the victim companies, posing as the supplier and asking for payment of invoices for goods and services actually provided by the real supplier. Narration: 'He emailed their staff fake invoices in its name.'",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "cnbc17",
    "cyberscoop",
    "africacheck"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_forged",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN FACT CARD: forged invoices, contracts and letters that falsely appeared to have been executed and signed by executives and agents of the victim companies (and bore false corporate stamps) were submitted to banks to support the transfers. Card: 'HE FORGED SIGNATURES OF / THEIR OWN EXECUTIVES.'",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "cnbc17",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_split",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Per a 2017 Lithuanian court order (reported by NBC/Reuters) Google was defrauded of about $23 million and Facebook of about $99 million; Bloomberg's source: about $23 million and about $98 million. Shown as the WIRED chip ticking $23M then $120M+ in s04 (no narration of the split).",
   "sources": [
    "nbc",
    "bloomberg",
    "register"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_lesson",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LESSON CARD (for the viewer): before paying an invoice or sending money on an emailed request, (1) verify it by phone on a number you already have, never one given in the email; (2) check the sender's exact address/domain for look-alikes; (3) treat urgency and changed bank details as red flags and stop. Rows: 'CALL A NUMBER YOU KNOW', 'CHECK THE EXACT ADDRESS', 'URGENT? NEW BANK? STOP.' Credit: 'FBI · Action Fraud / UK police'.",
   "sources": [
    "fbi_pdf",
    "fbi_phx",
    "nbcc",
    "psni"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_stat",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "STATISTIC (counter): in 2025, business email compromise caused $3,046,598,558 in reported losses (24,768 complaints), the second-costliest crime type in the FBI IC3 2025 Internet Crime Report (released April 2026). Narration: 'Last year, this scam cost victims 3 billion dollars.' (card: REPORTED LOSSES, 2025)",
   "sources": [
    "ic3_jds",
    "ic3_mh",
    "ic3_ib"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_sentence",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "He pleaded guilty to wire fraud in March 2019 (agreeing to forfeit about $49.7 million) and in December 2019 was sentenced in Manhattan federal court to five years in prison, with forfeiture of $49,738,559.41 and restitution of $26,479,079.24. Narration: 'He got five years in prison.' (card: DEC 2019)",
   "sources": [
    "doj_sent",
    "secaff",
    "cyberscoop",
    "computing"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "F_twist",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "reported",
   "text": "IRONY / TWIST (characterisation): the fraud relied on impersonation by email (a company registered under a copied name and phishing emails), not on breaking into computer systems. Narration: 'His weapon? A copied name and an email.'",
   "sources": [
    "doj_plea",
    "cnbc19",
    "africacheck"
   ]
  }
 ],
